---
title: Rebuilding BumbleBeing
blurb: Forty-one decisions behind a portfolio rebuild, including the seven I argued against and lost.
date: 2026-09-30
tags: [web]
---

This site used to be a SvelteKit project with eighteen vendored component
directories, a collapsible sidebar, three project pages that shared one placeholder
description verbatim, and a navigation flower whose petals linked to routes that did
not exist. It worked, mostly. It did not showcase anything.

So I rebuilt it — same architecture, same palette, nothing else assumed. Before
writing a line of code I had the whole design interrogated question by question:
forty-one of them across five rounds, each with a recommendation attached that I
could take or reject. This is what came out, and where the interesting arguments
were.

## Audit first, opinions second

The most useful hour was spent not designing but *measuring the old site*, and
several things it turned up contradicted what I believed about my own project.

I described the palette from memory as "light beige and beige". It was white
(`oklch(1 0 0)`) and a saturated honey amber, with beige appearing in exactly one
place: the sidebar. The honeycomb background I was rather pleased with had its two
cell colours **1.97% apart in lightness** — effectively invisible on any screen. And
the site loaded Galdeano, a font with a single 400 weight, then applied
`font-extrabold` to every project title, so the entire site had been rendering
synthesised faux-bold.

None of those were visible from the inside. All three were obvious the moment
somebody read the CSS back to me.

## The argument I lost, and should have

Curating the project roster was the decision I pushed back hardest on and got
wrong. My instinct was to show eight projects; the site now shows four. Four is
better. Three ML projects and one systems project read as *an ML and systems
engineer*, where eight read as somebody who has done a lot of unrelated things. The
narrower claim is the stronger one.

## The arguments I won

Seven decisions went against the recommendation I was given, and I would take most
of them again:

- **Old URLs 404 on purpose.** No redirects. The themed error page names the path
  you missed and points at `/projects`, which I prefer to a silent bounce.
- **The buzz effect is canvas, not CSS.** Advised against, on grounds of cost. It
  cost one shared canvas and a loop that stops when nothing is hovered.
- **`game` ships as a tag with zero projects behind it**, so the next one drops in
  without styling work.

The one I am least sure about: I kept the white background rather than moving to the
beige I originally described wanting. That one is still open, and it is now a
one-line change, which is the point of the next section.

## Two token layers

The old stylesheet defined about thirty variables twice — once for light, once for
dark — so every colour change meant two edits and the themes drifted apart. The new
one has three blocks: raw primitives (`--honey-500`, `--beige-100`), semantic roles
mapped onto them (`--surface`, `--ink`, `--primary`), and a dark block that
reassigns **only** the roles that actually differ. Components may use semantic names
only. A component reaching for `--honey-500` has broken the layering and dark mode
stops being a one-place change.

The payoff showed up immediately. "Mute the primary 10% toward the background" was
one line per theme, expressed as a `color-mix` against `--background` so it will
follow if the ground ever does become beige.

## Things that only looking at it could catch

Two bugs survived every amount of reasoning and died the instant something rendered.

The dark honeycomb **shouted**. Its lightness delta was correct and the tests
passed — but the cell colour carried nearly twice the chroma of its ground, so the
gaps rendered as bright red lines rather than texture. Lightness delta alone, it
turns out, is not a sufficient specification for a texture.

The buzzing dots were **invisible**. The canvas was painting: I confirmed 121
non-transparent pixels in the buffer. At 1.2px radius none of them were legible.

## What the rebuild actually bought

Adding a project used to mean editing the sidebar's hardcoded data array *and*
writing a new route that duplicated it. It is now one Markdown file. Frontmatter is
validated with Zod at build time, so a missing field or an unknown tag fails the
build naming the file and the field, rather than rendering `undefined` in
production.

Writing that validator produced my favourite small lesson of the whole rebuild.
`Date.parse('2025-02-31')` does not fail. It returns a perfectly valid timestamp for
the 3rd of March. And writing `date: 2026-09-30` in YAML frontmatter does not give
you that string either — YAML parses it into a `Date`, which the Markdown
preprocessor then compiles into `"2026-09-30T00:00:00.000Z"`. Three different
shapes, one of which is the obvious thing to type.

Both are handled now. Neither was something I would have thought to check.
